import Nivat.External.Colle.MinkowskiEdges

/-!
# `∑ z ∈ S, segOf z` is not `↑S` — a compiled guard against a signature bug

This file exists to keep one refutation permanently in the kernel's sight.  It has no
consumers by design; its job is to make a specific wrong statement impossible to re-introduce
without noticing.

## What it guards

Collé's reflected convex support is `𝒮_ψ := conv(-supp ψ) ∩ ℤ²` (`b3_colle2.txt:298`).
On 2026-09-17 a statement of Proposition 2.8 rendered that object as
`E (∑ z ∈ Sψ, segOf z)` — i.e. as the zonotope generated by treating each point of `Sψ` as a
vector out of the origin — instead of `E (↑Sψ)`.  Since `segOf z = {0, z}`
(`MinkowskiEdges.lean:192`) and `∑` is the Minkowski sum, the two sets genuinely differ.

**Nothing was wrong with any definition.** `supp`, `Conv`, `segOf`, `E` and `𝒮_ψ` were all
built correctly; the conclusion was simply aimed at the wrong object.  The `∑ segOf` shape is
*licensed* on the other side of that statement, where `φ = ∏ (X^{h_i} - 1)` really is a product
of binomials and `Conv_supp_prod_eq_Conv_zonoF` (`NewtonZonotope.lean:181-183`, Ostrowski)
applies.  It was copied across to `ψ`, an arbitrary annihilator, where no Newton-polygon
theorem permits it.  Copying the notation did not copy the licence.

## The refutation

`zero_mem_sum_segOf` is the whole content: every `segOf z` contains `0`, so the Minkowski sum
does too, unconditionally.  Hence any `S` with `0 ∉ S` separates the two sets — no convexity,
finiteness or genericity assumption needed.  `Sψ = {(1,0), (0,1), (1,1)}` is one witness.

See `blueprint/NOTE.md` 「定义债：prop_2_8 的结论打在错的对象上」 and CLAUDE.md
「签名落地协议」step 4 (廉价证伪), for which this is the worked example.
-/

open Pointwise

namespace Nivat.LE2

/-- The Minkowski sum of the segments `[0, z]` contains `0`, for any index set. -/
theorem zero_mem_sum_segOf (S : Finset (ℤ × ℤ)) :
    (0 : ℤ × ℤ) ∈ ∑ z ∈ S, segOf z := by
  classical
  induction S using Finset.induction with
  | empty => simp
  | insert a s ha ih =>
      rw [Finset.sum_insert ha]
      exact ⟨0, by simp [segOf], 0, ih, by simp⟩

/-- **The guard.**  If `0 ∉ S` then `∑ z ∈ S, segOf z` and `↑S` are different sets, so a
statement about `conv(S) ∩ ℤ²` may not be rendered as one about `∑ z ∈ S, segOf z`. -/
theorem sum_segOf_ne_coe (S : Finset (ℤ × ℤ)) (h0 : (0 : ℤ × ℤ) ∉ S) :
    (∑ z ∈ S, segOf z) ≠ (↑S : Set (ℤ × ℤ)) := by
  intro heq
  have h := zero_mem_sum_segOf S
  rw [heq] at h
  exact h0 (by simpa using h)

end Nivat.LE2
